==English==

===Etymology 1===
{{root|en|ine-pro|*(s)mer-|id=remember}}
From {{inh|en|enm|remembren}}, from {{der|en|fro|remembrer||to remember}}, from {{der|en|la-lat|rememorari||to remember again}}, from {{m|la|re-}} + {{m|la|memor||mindful}}, from {{der|en|ine-pro|*(s)mer-||to think about, be mindful, remember|id=remember}}. Cognate with {{cog|ang|mimorian}}, {{m|ang|mymerian||to remember, commemorate}}, {{cog|ang|māmorian||to deliberate, plan out, design}}. More at {{l|en|mimmer}} and {{l|en|mammer}}. Compare also dialectical {{noncog|en|umbethink}}.

{{col-top|2|etymology note}}
The success of the [[Old French]] word was helped by its proximity in sound and meaning to an existing Germanic word: {{cog|ang|mimorian}}, {{m|ang|mymerian||to remember, commemorate}} from {{cog|gem-pro|*mimrōną}}, {{m|gem-pro|*mīmrōną||to remember, be mindful}}, from the same Indo-European source, and is akin to {{cog|stq|miemerje||to ponder, reflect}}, {{cog|gml|mimeren||to ponder, meditate}}, {{cog|dum|mimeren||to reflect, think to oneself}} ({{cog|nl|mijmeren||to muse, reflect deeply}}), {{cog|ang|ġemimor||mindful}}, {{cog|non|Mímir}}, {{m|non|Mim||Norse god of memory}}, {{cog|ang|māmrian||to think out, design}}. Related to {{l|en|mourn}}.

Displaced native {{cog|enm|ȝemuneȝen||to remember}}, from {{cog|ang|ġemynegian||to remember, remind}}; {{cog|enm|minnen||to remember, have in mind}}, from {{cog|non|minna||to remind}}; {{cog|enm|munden}}, {{m|enm|ȝemunden||to bear in mind, remember}}, from {{cog|ang|ġemynd||memory, remembrance}}; {{cog|enm|ithenchen}}, {{m|enm|ȝethenchen||to think on, remember}}, from {{cog|ang|ġeþencan}}; {{cog|enm|manien||to remind, mention, remember}}, from {{cog|ang|manian||to admonish, remind, mention}}.
{{col-bottom}}

